// Copy to secrets.h — in THIS folder, beside door_gate.ino — and fill in all
// four values before flashing. secrets.h is
// gitignored. Gate 2.0.0 adds one more at the bottom — the setup portal's
// password — so it is five in all.
//
// This header is REQUIRED to build `door_gate`, and every value in it is
// mandatory: main.cpp `#error`s on a missing one and `static_assert`s on an
// empty one. That is deliberate — an image that joins an empty SSID, or posts
// to an empty base URL with an empty bearer key, looks identical to a working
// one until it is screwed to a wall beside a door, and there is no runtime way
// to correct it.
//
// THE FIRST FOUR ARE FIRST-BOOT DEFAULTS ONLY (gate 2.0.0, door-gate.md §14).
// A rotated device key arrives over the heartbeat; the WiFi and the server
// address change from the console's Club network panel or at the setup portal
// (hold the BOOT button five seconds within a minute of power-on). Changing
// them here and reflashing is never needed for a new key, a new network or a
// moved server; a cable flash is only ever for a new image, such as
// the one that puts 2.0.0 on the board. See
// src/iot/door-gate/arduino/README.md.
//
// THE SAME FOUR VALUES FOR EVERY DOOR. Nothing per-gate belongs in here: a door
// is named in the admin console (`gate_key`), the API keys that name to the
// board's MAC, and the board never needs to know it — it does not read
// `gate_key` and has no field to keep one in. Two doors run byte-identical
// images and a spare board is a spare board rather than a board for one
// particular door.
#pragma once

// EVERY VALUE BELOW IS A PLACEHOLDER AND EVERY PLACEHOLDER IS A COMPILE ERROR.
//
// This file used to ship `WIFI_SSID "ClubWiFi"` and a real-looking LAN address
// — plausible values that compile, flash, and produce a board that joins
// nothing and posts to a machine that is not the club's. Nothing checked them,
// and the only feedback surface on this board is ONE LED, so "wrong SSID" and
// "server down" are the same slow blink to the person on the ladder.
//
// main.cpp `static_assert`s each of these four literals CHARACTER FOR
// CHARACTER. Changing one here without changing it there stops the guard
// firing on the file it exists to guard.

// The club WiFi. WPA2 personal; the board takes a DHCP lease and there is no
// static-IP option, deliberately — Carl's sketch hard-coded 192.168.68.151,
// which works exactly until somebody changes the router, the subnet, or fits a
// second door.
#define WIFI_SSID       "REPLACE_ME_WIFI_SSID"
#define WIFI_PASSWORD   "REPLACE_ME_WIFI_PASSWORD"

// Where the API is. THE BUILD REFUSES ANYTHING BUT `http://` + AN IPv4 LITERAL
// + AN OPTIONAL `:port`, with no trailing slash and no path — main.cpp
// static_asserts the whole grammar (gatelogic/ApiBaseUrl.h), so a mistake here
// is a compile error rather than a board on a wall that admits nobody. Each
// rule is a real failure:
//
//   * A HOSTNAME resolves through lwIP's DNS, which NEITHER HTTP timeout
//     bounds: about 16 s on the first server and about 15 s on the second. One
//     heartbeat against an unreachable resolver holds the loop for longer than
//     the watchdog, so the board resets, boots, heartbeats and resets again,
//     forever, having loaded no roster and admitted nobody.
//   * `https://` is ACCEPTED by the HTTP client against a plain socket and then
//     spoken as cleartext to port 443 — no error, no warning. The device API is
//     cleartext on the club LAN by design; a URL that claims otherwise is not.
//   * A TRAILING SLASH produces `//api/v1/device/gate`, a 404, which the gate
//     correctly reads as "learned nothing" and answers from its roster: a door
//     silently running offline-only against a healthy server.
//   * `0.0.0.0` — the value below — and `255.255.255.255` are refused as
//     reserved hosts. `0.0.0.0` is THIS FILE'S PLACEHOLDER, so an unedited copy
//     of it cannot become an image on a wall; `255.255.255.255` is the limited
//     broadcast address, which is not a request to the API and never will be.
//
// The board and the backend sit on the same club LAN, so this is the backend's
// LAN address — not the public tunnel hostname, which would send every scan out
// to Cloudflare and back while somebody stands at the door, and which this
// grammar refuses anyway.
// Replace 0.0.0.0 with the backend's LAN IPv4 address, e.g.
// "http://192.168.1.10:8088". The port is optional and defaults to 80.
#define API_BASE_URL    "http://0.0.0.0:8088"

// The tenant's device API key, from the admin Devices page. It is key `k0`, the
// one the board starts with; a rotation delivers the next over the heartbeat
// and the board keeps both, so no rotation is a reflash. It goes out as
// `Authorization: Bearer <key>` on every call.
//
// It matters even though the board never leaves the LAN: the same API is
// reachable from the internet through the Cloudflare tunnel, because that is
// how members book and how the admin works remotely.
#define DEVICE_API_KEY  "REPLACE_ME_DEVICE_API_KEY"

// The setup portal's WPA2 password (8 to 63 characters), printed on the label
// inside the enclosure beside the portal's name, CRUSADERS-SETUP-<last four of
// the MAC>.
#define SETUP_AP_PASSWORD "REPLACE_ME_SETUP_AP_PASSWORD"
